course: dictionary
lesson: 14462
title: Parity: equal or even
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Parity means being equal. Two things have parity when they are the same in a way that matters, such as pay, rank or chances. «Men and women should have parity of pay» means both should get the same pay.
explain: In maths, parity tells you whether a whole number is even or odd. «6 and 8 have the same parity» because both are even. «6 and 7 have different parity» because one is even and one is odd.
explain: Look for clue words like equal, same, fair or balance. If the sentence is about fairness, try swapping in «equal» or «the same as». If it still makes sense, parity probably means that. Parity is a formal word, so it often appears in exams, news and maths.
fact: Parity means being equal, or having the same level, especially in pay, rank or chances.
fact: In maths, parity tells you whether a whole number is even or odd.
fact: Even plus even is always even, odd plus odd is even, and odd plus even is odd.
fact: «The pound and the euro reached parity» means the two currencies became worth the same.
fact: In computers, a parity bit is a simple check that helps find mistakes in data.
exercise: «Men and women should have parity of pay.» What does parity mean here? | Pay given only in summer | Lower pay for everyone | *Equal pay | Pay that changes every day
exercise: «6 and 7 have different parity.» Do 6 and 7 have the same parity? | *No, one is even and one is odd | Yes, both are odd | Yes, both are even | Yes, both are whole numbers
exercise: Ms Patel asked for «parity between the two teams». What does parity suggest here? | A loud noise | *A fair balance | Wet weather | Something very old
test: What is odd plus odd? | Zero | *Even | Prime | Odd
test: «A computer uses a parity bit.» What is it for? | To play music | *To help find mistakes in data | To make the computer faster | To change the colour of the screen
exam: «The two teams finished the match on parity, with the same score.» What does parity mean here? | Hidden | Broken | *Equal | Different
